course: world
lesson: 20
title: Clever reasoning about water and matter
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Most liquids shrink when they freeze, but water is unusual: it expands, so ice is less dense than water and floats.
explain: Because ice floats, a pond freezes from the top down. The ice lid insulates the water below, which stays liquid, so fish and frogs can survive the winter at the bottom.
explain: A cold glass gets wet on the outside because water vapour in the warm air touches the cold glass, cools and condenses: the water comes from the air, not through the glass.
explain: Salt is spread on icy roads because salty water freezes at a lower temperature than pure water; the salt dissolves in the thin wet layer on the ice and makes it melt even a few degrees below 0 °C.
explain: Good reasoning asks: where does the water or the heat come from, where does it go, and what would happen if things were different?
fact: Water expands by about one tenth when it freezes.
fact: Ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats.
fact: Water is densest at about 4 °C, so in winter the deepest water of a lake is often about 4 °C while the top freezes.
fact: Ice and snow are good insulators and slow down the cooling of whatever is under them.
fact: Salty water freezes at a lower temperature than fresh water.
fact: In very hard frost road salt stops working, because even salty water freezes when it is cold enough.
fact: The drops on the outside of a cold glass come from water vapour in the air.
fact: In very dry air a cold glass gets far fewer drops on it than in damp air.
fact: Melting ice takes in heat from around it, which is how ice cools a drink.
fact: When floating ice melts, the water level hardly changes, because the ice was already pushing aside its own weight of water.
exercise: Why does ice float on water? | Water expands when it freezes, so ice is less dense | Ice is full of air bubbles that hold it up | Ice is heavier, but the cold water underneath pushes it up | Ice floats because it is colder than the water
exercise: Why can fish live through the winter in a frozen pond? | The ice on top insulates the liquid water below | Fish freeze solid and come back to life in spring | The pond freezes from the bottom, leaving water on top | Fish make enough heat to melt the ice
exercise: What could happen to lakes in cold countries if ice sank instead of floating? | They could freeze solid from the bottom up | They would never freeze at all | The fish would be warmer in winter | The ice would melt faster down at the bottom of the lake
exercise: Where does the water on the outside of a glass of iced drink come from? | Water vapour in the air condensing on the cold glass | The drink seeping out through the glass | The ice melting and seeping slowly through the glass wall | The glass sweating like skin
exercise: Why is salt spread on icy roads? | Salty water freezes at a lower temperature than pure water | Salt is warm and heats the road | Salt makes the ice rough so tyres grip | Salt soaks up all of the water out of the ice and leaves it dry
exercise: Why does road salt stop working in very hard frost? | Even salty water freezes when it is cold enough | Salt itself freezes solid in winter | Salt only works in daylight | Cars blow the salt away when it is cold
exercise: How does an ice cube make a drink cold? | Melting ice takes heat from the drink | The ice gives its cold to the drink | The ice makes the drink heavier | Cold flows out of the ice like a liquid
exercise: A cold glass gets dripping wet in a damp summer kitchen, but hardly wet at all in a dry desert. Why? | Desert air holds very little water vapour | Glasses in the desert are made differently | The glass stays warmer in the desert | The desert sand soaks up the drops at once
exercise: A full milk bottle is left on a frosty doorstep and its cap is pushed up. Why? | The freezing milk expands and pushes upward | Frost pulls the cap off from the outside at night | The glass bottle shrinks and squeezes the milk up | The cold air inside the bottle expands and lifts it
exercise: Floating sea ice melts. What happens to the sea level? | It hardly changes, because the ice already pushed aside its own weight of water | It rises by the full height of the ice | It falls, because ice takes up more space | It rises a great deal, just as it does when the ice on land melts into the sea
exercise: In a freezing winter, pipes crack while the water inside is frozen, but the leak is usually found only after the thaw. Why? | The ice plugs the crack until it melts | Pipes only crack when they warm up | Warm water is heavier and breaks pipes | Thawing makes the metal shrink and split
exercise: In winter the water at the bottom of a deep lake is about 4 °C while the surface freezes. Why? | Water at about 4 °C is densest and sinks to the bottom | Warm water always sinks below cold water | Sunlight warms the bottom of the lake first | The fish keep the bottom warm with their bodies
exercise: Why do people also scatter sand or grit on icy paths? | Grit makes the surface rough so shoes can grip | Grit melts the ice much faster than salt ever does | Grit makes the ice thicker, so it is stronger to walk on | Grit soaks up all of the cold that is in the ice
exercise: You fill an ice cube tray right to the brim and freeze it. What do the cubes look like? | They bulge up above the edges | They sink below the edges | They stay exactly level with the edges | They break up into powder
test: A sealed plastic bottle filled to the top with water is put in the freezer. What happens? | It bulges or splits as the ice expands | It is squeezed inward as the water shrinks | Nothing changes at all | The water stays liquid because the bottle is sealed
test: A girl says, "My glass of cold lemonade is leaking, it is wet outside." What would show she is wrong? | An empty cold sealed jar beside it gets wet too | Adding more ice makes it wetter | Wiping the glass dries it for a moment | Pouring the lemonade into a bigger glass
test: If ice were denser than water, what might happen to pond life in a long hard winter? | Ponds could freeze solid and many animals would die | Ponds would never freeze over | Fish could swim up and live on top of the ice instead | Nothing would change for them
test: How can salt melt the ice on a road at -5 °C? | Salty water freezes below -5 °C, so it stays liquid | Salt heats the ice up to 0 °C | Salt is a fuel that slowly burns | Car tyres warm the salt up
test: In the same cold weather, a lake freezes over but the sea beside it does not freeze. What is the best explanation? | Seawater is salty and freezes at a lower temperature | Seawater is always warmer from the Sun | Seawater is heavier than lake water, so it cannot freeze | Fish in the sea keep the water warm
test: Why does a pond freeze first at the top and not at the bottom? | The surface touches the cold air, and ice floats | Ice sinks and piles up from the bottom | The Sun warms only the top of the pond | Frogs on the bottom melt the ice there
test: Ice cubes float in a glass of water filled exactly to the brim. What happens as they melt? | The water level stays about the same and it does not overflow | It overflows, because the ice turns into more water | The level drops well below the brim | It overflows only if the cubes were big
test: Why does a mountain hut buried under deep snow stay warmer inside than the frosty air? | The snow traps air and insulates the hut | The snow makes heat as it sits there | Snow is made of warm water | The snow pulls the cold out of the walls
test: Why does your breath make a white cloud on a frosty day but not on a warm day? | Cold air cools the vapour in your breath so it condenses into droplets | In winter breath has smoke in it | In summer we breathe out no water at all | Cold air freezes your breath at once and turns it into tiny flakes of snow
test: why do ice cubes float in ur drink | Ice is less dense than liquid water | Ice has air bubbles that make it a gas | Ice is lighter because it is cold | The drink pushes all solids to the top
test: if the ice in my glass melts will it overflow | No, the level hardly changes | Yes, melted ice makes much more water | Yes, it will spill over the top | No, the level drops a lot
test: Leo says the drops on a cold glass leak through from inside. true? | No, they come from water vapour in the air | Yes, cold glass lets water through | Yes, the drink sweats through tiny holes | No, they come from the ice melting outside
test: my brother says road salt melts ice however cold it is. right? | No, in very hard frost even salty water freezes | Yes, salt melts ice at any temperature | Yes, the colder it is the better salt works | No, salt only works on snow, not ice
exam: Identify the principal reason for which ice floats upon liquid water. | Ice is less dense than liquid water | Ice is heavier than the liquid water below it | Ice contains trapped air and nothing else | Ice is warmer than the liquid water beneath
exam: It is given that a lake freezes at its surface in winter. Ascertain the usual temperature of its deepest water. | Approximately four degrees Celsius | Zero degrees Celsius, like the ice above | Minus ten degrees Celsius, or colder still | Twenty degrees Celsius, as in summer
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the drops upon the exterior of a cold glass. | They derive from water vapour present in the surrounding air | They have passed through the wall of the glass | They have been produced by the cold itself | They have splashed from the beverage within
exam: Whereas floating ice melts in a glass of water, determine the effect upon the water level. | It remains approximately unaltered | It rises markedly as the ice turns to water | It falls markedly as the ice disappears | It invariably overflows the rim of the vessel
